====================================================================
(A) #perfect matchings of 2n points = (2n-1)!! = product of ODDS (so ODD)
====================================================================
  2n | #matchings | (2n-1)!! | odd? | 2^n*(1/2)^(n) [rising 1/2, S644]
   2 |          1 |        1 | True | 2^1*(1/2)^(1)=1 -> =1? True
   4 |          3 |        3 | True | 2^2*(1/2)^(2)=3 -> =3? True
   6 |         15 |       15 | True | 2^3*(1/2)^(3)=15 -> =15? True
   8 |        105 |      105 | True | 2^4*(1/2)^(4)=105 -> =105? True
  10 |        945 |      945 | True | 2^5*(1/2)^(5)=945 -> =945? True
  12 |      10395 |    10395 | True | 2^6*(1/2)^(6)=1.04e+04 -> =10395? True
  -> the Pfaffian term-count (2n-1)!! is ODD; = 2^n * rising-factorial-of-1/2 (S644).

====================================================================
(B) WHY the tournament Pfaffian is ODD: odd-many +-1 terms
====================================================================
  n=2: Pfaffian values [1]; all ODD? True; det==Pf^2 ? True
  n=4: Pfaffian values [1, 3]; all ODD? True; det==Pf^2 ? True
  n=6: Pfaffian values [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]; all ODD? True; det==Pf^2 ? True
  -> every +-1 skew Pfaffian is ODD (odd # of +-1 terms); det = Pf^2 exactly.

====================================================================
(C) the PARITY MASTER SWITCH and even rank of skew matrices
====================================================================
  n | matching exists? | Pfaffian | det | rank | rank even?
  1 |              no |        0 |    0 |    0 | True
  2 |             yes |        1 |    1 |    2 | True
  3 |              no |        0 |    0 |    2 | True
  4 |             yes |        1 |    1 |    4 | True
  5 |              no |        0 |    0 |    4 | True
  6 |             yes |        1 |    1 |    6 | True
  7 |              no |        0 |    0 |    6 | True
  -> even n: matching exists, Pf!=0, det=Pf^2 square, full rank n (even).
     odd n: NO perfect matching, Pf=0, det=0, rank n-1 (even). Skew rank ALWAYS even.
     The even/odd of n is the master switch; the Pfaffian lives only on the even side,
     and the n->n-2 Pfaffian recursion stays within one parity class (Mode B, S645).
